WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.794
Room 4 is free from twelve to one.

2
00:00:04.794 --> 00:00:12.740
Two people book it at the same moment: a settlement review from twelve to one, and a fraud sync from twelve thirty to one thirty.

3
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.960
Each app checks first: does any booking of room 4 overlap mine?

4
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.084
It even adds FOR UPDATE, the row lock that stopped lost updates.

5
00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:22.951
Both checks find no rows.

6
00:00:22.951 --> 00:00:28.811
A row lock is a mark on a row that exists, so they lock nothing, and neither one waits.

7
00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:31.045
Both insert, and both commit.

8
00:00:31.045 --> 00:00:33.889
Twelve thirty to one is now booked twice.

9
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:40.718
Each new booking is a phantom to the other: a row that matches its search but didn't exist when it searched.

10
00:00:40.718 --> 00:00:42.924
A row lock can't protect an absence.

11
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.452
First fix: give the conflict a row.

12
00:00:45.452 --> 00:00:50.964
Each booking first locks room 4's own row in the rooms table, which always exists.

13
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.908
The second booker waits there.

14
00:00:52.908 --> 00:00:58.452
When the first commits, the second one's check runs and sees the new booking, so it refuses.

15
00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:00.957
This is materializing the conflict.

16
00:01:00.957 --> 00:01:04.940
It holds only if every code path remembers to lock the room.

17
00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:08.602
Better: state the rule and let the database enforce it.

18
00:01:08.602 --> 00:01:12.955
An exclusion constraint says no two bookings of one room may overlap.

19
00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:16.822
Its check looks at every row in the index, committed or not.

20
00:01:16.822 --> 00:01:21.756
The second insert finds the first, waits for it, and fails the moment it commits.

21
00:01:21.933 --> 00:01:27.550
When the rule is arithmetic across rows, such as a sum of refunds, run at SERIALIZABLE.

22
00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:33.232
PostgreSQL notes what each check read, fails one commit, and the retry sees the booking.

23
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:34.642
A lock needs a row.

24
00:01:34.642 --> 00:01:43.333
When the conflict is about a row that doesn't exist yet, lock a row that stands for it, or let a constraint or SERIALIZABLE catch it.
